It depends on the variant. Yes, they should be noted; but many variants
have negligible impact on difficulty, or make the game more difficult.

On my home installation, for example, I play with the heck2 patch, the
credit clone prevention patch, the nested bag patch, and the dump
patch. The dump patch obviously has no direct effect on gameplay;
although it's arguable that if you use it consistently, you can glean a
bit more information than you would have otherwise concerning specific
luck scores, etc., which could affect strategy on later games.

The heck2 patch has probably a negligible effect on difficulty. It
mainly takes some of the tediousness out of the gehennom mazes; on the
other hand, many of the special levels have more open spaces that allow
more monsters to attack you at once, so if anything, it's probably
harder.

The credit clone prevention patch definitely makes the game harder,
although it's negligible for me, because I rarely credit clone to begin
with.

The nested looting patch is, IMO, mostly a game interface convenience.
When the patch was developed, there was discussion in this group that
it made the game easier because you could get to an inner bag in one
turn and without taking it out of an outer bag. However, since then,
pickup exceptions has been implemented, which certainly saves many more
game turns than nested looting ever would.

Now for completeness, if I were to post YAAP, I would mention these
patches. However, to suggest that an ascension using them was "not
nethack" or incomparable to nethack, to me, would be a gross
overstatement.

